        option_a: `In sentence 1, "to advise younger Scholars" should be "advising younger Scholars" to match the gerund forms; "the regular inspection" should be "regularly inspecting the Eastern Stacks." In sentence 2, "performing work with diligence" should be "diligence."`,
        option_b: `In sentence 1, "to advise younger Scholars" should be "advising younger Scholars"; "the regular inspection of the Eastern Stacks" should be "regularly inspecting the Eastern Stacks." In sentence 2, "performing work with diligence" should be "diligent work."`,
        option_c: `In sentence 1, "maintaining the catalogue" should be "to maintain the catalogue" and "to advise" is correct; "the regular inspection" should be "to inspect the Eastern Stacks regularly." In sentence 2, "performing work with diligence" should be "diligence."`,
        option_d: `In sentence 1, "to advise younger Scholars" should be "advising younger Scholars" to match the gerund forms; "the regular inspection of the Eastern Stacks" should be "inspecting the Eastern Stacks regularly." In sentence 2, "performing work with diligence" should be "diligence."`,
        correct_answer: 'D',
        explanation: `Sentence 1 lists three items after the colon: "maintaining the catalogue of restricted scrolls" (gerund phrase), "to advise younger Scholars on matters of grammar" (infinitive phrase — mismatch), and "the regular inspection of the Eastern Stacks" (noun phrase — mismatch). All three should be gerund phrases: "maintaining... advising... inspecting the Eastern Stacks regularly." Option D correctly identifies both non-parallel forms and converts them to gerunds. Sentence 2 lists "precision, consistency, and performing work with diligence." The first two are abstract nouns; the third is a gerund phrase. It should be "diligence" (an abstract noun). Option A identifies the gerund fix correctly for sentence 1 but proposes "diligent work" (an adjective-noun phrase) for sentence 2 instead of the abstract noun "diligence." Option B is close but proposes "diligent work" for sentence 2, same problem. Option C incorrectly proposes changing "maintaining" to "to maintain," altering the correct first item unnecessarily.`,
