        correct_answer: 'B',
        explanation: `Each sentence has a correlative conjunction parallel error. Sentence 1: "not only respected by her colleagues" (passive adjective phrase) "but also the younger Scholars admired her" (independent clause with different subject) — mismatch. Fix: "but also admired by the younger Scholars" (parallel passive adjective phrase). Sentence 2: "either you learn grammar through careful study" (subject-verb clause) "or by practicing it obsessively" (prepositional phrase) — mismatch. Fix: "or you practice it obsessively" (parallel subject-verb clause). Sentence 3: "both that mastery requires patience" (noun clause with "that") "and to never stop revising" (infinitive phrase) — mismatch. Fix: "and that you never stop revising" (parallel noun clause with "that"). Option B correctly identifies all three. Option A proposes "never to stop revising" for sentence 3, which merely moves the adverb "never" but does not fix the that-clause vs. infinitive mismatch. Option C proposes restructuring sentence 1 to an active clause, which is parallel but unnecessarily changes both halves. Option D proposes "practicing it obsessively" (gerund, not a clause) for sentence 2, which still does not match "you learn grammar through careful study."`,
