        explanation: `Sentence 1: The "not only...but also" structure sets up: "not only more thorough than the previous draft but also [list]." The list after "but also" contains: "organized more clearly" (adjective/adverb phrase), "argued more persuasively" (verb phrase), and "including new evidence" (present participle — mismatch). The list items after "but also" are parallel adjective/verb phrases; "including" breaks the pattern. Fix: "included new evidence" (past tense verb matching the implied parallel structure of "organized" and "argued" — all modifying the proposal as past tense adjective-verb descriptions). Sentence 2: "both [X] and [Y]" — X is "a stronger command of the subject matter" (noun phrase), Y is "that she had improved her ability to present arguments" (noun clause with "that" — mismatch). Fix: "an improved ability to present arguments" (noun phrase matching "a stronger command"). Option A correctly identifies both. Option B incorrectly proposes changing the noun phrase in sentence 2 to a "that" clause, making the mismatch worse. Option D proposes "incorporating" for sentence 1 — this still uses a present participle and does not fix the parallel break.`,
